# ============================================================
# OneProMag — what must never be committed
# ============================================================
# The rule this file follows: anything that can hold a credential, a session,
# a real client's data or a transcript of our own work is ignored by pattern,
# not by habit. The app's source, schema and demo seed ARE tracked — the demo
# passwords in server/db.js are fixtures for an empty database, not secrets.

# ---- 1. Secrets and local configuration --------------------------------
# Real credentials live in the environment, never in the tree: docker-compose
# reads POSTGRES_PASSWORD / PGPASSWORD from .env or the shell.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
secrets/
.secrets

# ---- 2. Databases, dumps and anything a "backup" command writes ---------
# The SQLite fallback writes data/app.db (+ WAL). The Postgres data dir is a
# named Docker volume, but a bind mount (./pgdata) would land here.
data/
pgdata/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
# Dumps sit in the repo root by convention: pg_dump > onepromag-2026-10-26.sql
/*.sql
*.sql.gz
*.dump
*.backup
backups/
# The schema itself is source, not data — never ignore it.
!server/schema/*.sql

# ---- 3. Session material ------------------------------------------------
# Sessions are signed cookies stored in the database, so nothing on disk holds
# one — except the tooling below, which quotes them verbatim.
.pi/
.cookies
cookies.txt

# ---- 4. Client data -----------------------------------------------------
# Uploaded/exported engagement data, if someone ever drops it beside the code.
uploads/
exports/
clients-data/

# ---- 5. Dependencies, build and cache -----------------------------------
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
.cache/
coverage/
dist/
build/

# ---- 6. Logs, editors, operating systems --------------------------------
*.log
logs/
*.tmp
*~
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
